<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Tadjoura</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1896 CE (approx.) · capital Tadjoura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Afar sultanate on the Gulf of Tadjoura that controlled caravan routes to the Ethiopian highlands and became part of French Djibouti.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tadjoura, one of the oldest towns of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/djibouti">Djibouti</a> coast, was the seat of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> sultanate from at least the 16th century. Its rulers came from Afar clans of the region and drew their power from the port, which linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> with the interior.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan ruled with clan elders over pastoral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> communities. Tadjoura's wealth came from caravans bringing slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and salt from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Shewa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> to the coast, and from its role as a stopping place for Arab traders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was well established, and the sultans dealt with Ottoman, Egyptian and later European powers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1884 the sultan signed a treaty placing Tadjoura under French protection, and in 1896 it became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-somaliland">French Somaliland</a>. The sultanate survives as a traditional office, and Tadjoura remains a regional capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/djibouti">Djibouti</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-tadjoura.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Tadjoura" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Driss 19:28, 3. Jul. 2007 (CEST) · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tagore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts